          "The mathematical foundation of continuous mean-reverting processes is the Ornstein-Uhlenbeck (OU) Stochastic Differential Equation: $dX_t = \\theta (\\mu - X_t) dt + \\sigma dW_t$.",
          "Here, $\\theta > 0$ represents the Speed of Mean Reversion, measuring how strongly the process is pulled back toward its equilibrium mean $\\mu$.",
          "The term $\\sigma$ represents the volatility of the Wiener process driving stochastic diffusion, and $dW_t$ is standard Brownian motion.",
          "The expected value of the process at time $t$ conditioned on initial state $X_0$ is: $E[X_t | X_0] = \\mu + (X_0 - \\mu) e^{-\\theta t}$.",
          "As time $t \\to \\infty$, the expected value converges asymptotically to $\\mu$, while the unconditional variance stabilizes at $\\text{Var}(X_\\infty) = \\frac{\\sigma^2}{2\\theta}$.",
          "Understanding the stationary distribution of the OU process allows quantitative traders to establish rigorous probabilistic entry and exit thresholds.",
          "In statistical arbitrage, precision calibration of mean reversion half-life prevents portfolio managers from holding diverging pairs past their statistical convergence window."
