        "say": [
          "To apply the Ornstein-Uhlenbeck model to discrete financial time series, we discretize the SDE into an Autoregressive Model of Order 1, AR(1).",
          "Applying Euler discretization with time step $\\Delta t$ yields: $X_t = a + b X_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$, where $\\epsilon_t \\sim N(0, \\sigma_\\epsilon^2)$.",
          "Comparing coefficients with the continuous OU process provides the calibration mapping: $b = e^{-\\theta \\Delta t} \\implies \\theta = -\\frac{\\ln(b)}{\\Delta t}$.",
          "The equilibrium mean is recovered as: $\\mu = \\frac{a}{1 - b}$, and the continuous volatility is: $\\sigma = \\sigma_\\epsilon \\sqrt{\\frac{-2 \\ln(b)}{\\Delta t (1 - b^2)}}$.",
          "The most intuitive practical metric in pairs trading is the Half-Life of Mean Reversion ($t_{1/2}$), the time required for a spread deviation to decay by 50%.",
          "The half-life equation is simply: $t_{1/2} = \\frac{\\ln(2)}{\\theta}$.",
          "If an estimated half-life is 3 days, a statistical arbitrageur knows that trading horizons should be structured around 3-to-6 day holding periods.",
          "Propagator models formalize price impact as a transient convolution between past signed order flow and a decaying response kernel, capturing the slow relaxation of supply-demand imbalances back toward baseline market equilibrium. Power-law decay kernels accurately mirror observed market memory profiles."
        ],
        "example": "Radioactive isotope decay where physics measures the half-life of atoms: financial spreads decay back to their baseline with identical exponential math.",
        "code": "import math\n\nclass OUCalibrator:\n    @staticmethod\n    def calibrate_from_ar1(a_intercept, b_slope, residual_variance, dt=1.0):\n        if b_slope >= 1.0 or b_slope <= 0:\n            return {\"valid\": False, \"reason\": \"NON_STATIONARY_SERIES\"}\n            \n        # Continuous parameters from discrete AR(1)\n        theta = -math.log(b_slope) / dt\n        mu = a_intercept / (1.0 - b_slope)\n        half_life = math.log(2.0) / theta\n        \n        sigma_sq = residual_variance * (-2.0 * math.log(b_slope)) / (dt * (1.0 - (b_slope ** 2)))\n        sigma = math.sqrt(max(0.0, sigma_sq))\n        \n        return {\n            \"valid\": True,\n            \"theta_speed\": round(theta, 4),\n            \"mu_equilibrium\": round(mu, 4),\n            \"half_life_days\": round(half_life, 2),\n            \"continuous_sigma\": round(sigma, 4)\n        }\n\n# Simulated discrete AR(1) calibration on equity spread: a = 15.0, b = 0.85 (dt = 1 day)\ncalib = OUCalibrator.calibrate_from_ar1(15.0, 0.85, 0.64, dt=1.0)\nprint(\"calibration valid:\", calib[\"valid\"])\nprint(\"mean reversion speed (theta):\", calib[\"theta_speed\"])\nprint(\"equilibrium mean (mu): $\", calib[\"mu_equilibrium\"])\nprint(\"half-life of spread:\", calib[\"half_life_days\"], \"trading days\")",
